const makeAug14Depth = (slug: string, title: string): string[] => {
  const [focus, measure, examples, warning, source] = august14ResearchDepth[slug];
  return [
    `Research question and scope: this report examines ${focus} for teams considering bounded support from a Filipino assistant. It does not attempt to rank workers, predict a universal result, or describe every outsourced arrangement. The unit of analysis is ${measure}. A manager should specify the queue, observation period, source records, reviewer, and exclusions before interpreting a result. Those fields matter because two teams can use the same label while measuring different work. A meaningful conclusion must stay at the level supported by the evidence: a task definition, a sampled process, or a local management decision.`,
    `Why the distinction matters: the practical comparison is among ${examples}. These activities can share a title but differ in data sensitivity, number of systems, interruption rate, and reversibility. Before assignment, write the expected input, allowed action, finish condition, owner, and stop rule. The assistant may prepare, classify, organize, or draft within that boundary; the accountable team keeps decisions that change commitments, policy, payment, access, or customer rights. This division makes review observable and prevents a successful low-risk sample from being treated as proof that a higher-risk queue is ready.`,
    `Method: start with a small, dated sample that contains ordinary cases and deliberately chosen edge cases. Ordinary items reveal whether the basic instruction can be followed. Edge cases reveal whether the stop condition is visible when a source is missing, a record conflicts, or the request changes. Record accepted, returned, escalated, and owner-overridden items separately. An escalation is not automatically a failure: it can show that the boundary is working. An unrecorded guess is harder to detect and should be treated as a control concern.`,
    `Evidence design: keep a compact ledger for every sampled item. Record the source identifier, received date, work category, permitted action, result, reviewer decision, and reason for return or escalation. When a number is used, include its definition, denominator, period, unit, and source URL. Separate a reported fact from a calculation and from an interpretation. ${source} offers useful principles for its subject area, but it does not establish a universal benchmark for OutsourcedAssistants.com or for one client’s queue.`,
    `Managerial load: measure the work required to make the support useful, not only the items completed. Include time to clarify the request, inspect evidence, correct a result, decide an exception, and update the instruction. A queue that appears productive may still consume more management capacity if every item needs reconstruction. Conversely, occasional escalations may be healthy when they are timely, complete, and directed to the right owner. Compare the pattern over a defined period and distinguish a one-off mistake from a recurring design problem.`,
    `Interpretation: findings should be stated as conditional observations. For example, a narrow queue with stable inputs and a responsive reviewer may be suitable for an initial assignment; that does not imply the same fit for a variable queue, a different tool set, or a period with heavy absences. A result can support a change to the brief, a new example, a narrower permission, a different review sample, or a pause. It cannot support claims about character, nationality, guaranteed quality, or universal availability.`,
    `Risk boundary: use individual accounts, least-privilege access, approved storage, and a named owner for exceptions. Do not copy unnecessary personal information into handoff notes. Protect financial approvals, sensitive account changes, legal interpretation, professional advice, and new commercial commitments. When an item crosses the boundary, preserve only the minimum context needed for routing and stop the unsupported action. The owner should decide whether the rule changes; the assistant should not widen authority through repetition or informal precedent.`,
    `Comparison table in words: compare the queue on input stability, action reversibility, exception frequency, review effort, and consequence of error. Input stability asks whether the source arrives in a predictable form. Reversibility asks whether an owner can safely undo the action. Exception frequency asks how often the written rule is insufficient. Review effort asks how much context is needed to verify the result. Consequence of error asks who could be affected and how quickly the issue can be contained. These dimensions are more useful than calling a task easy or strategic.`,
    `Limitations: a small operational sample may miss seasonal demand, language differences, tool outages, policy changes, and rare but important cases. Source records may contain legacy errors. Reviewers may disagree about what counts as complete. A time-zone observation may be confounded by meeting schedules; a quality sample may be confounded by unusually clear instructions. State the population, dates, exclusions, and unresolved questions. Do not generalize from one team to every Filipino assistant, one platform to every platform, or one client experience to a national workforce.`,
    `A useful second test changes one condition at a time. Change the deadline, input format, reviewer, system, or exception and inspect whether the same rule still works. If the answer depends on private background knowledge, add an example or explicit escalation field. If the result changes because the source changed, record the source-freshness rule. If it changes because review was unavailable, record the coverage dependency. This turns a vague concern into a concrete design question without pretending that a pilot proves causation.`,
    `Publication and review implication: explain which claims are sourced, which are calculated, which are observed in a local sample, and which are recommendations. Cite multiple claim-relevant authorities, but do not use a general security, labor, accessibility, or search guideline as proof of a business outcome. Keep the topic distinct from practical Blog advice by focusing on evidence, definitions, measurement, limitations, and decision boundaries. A reader should be able to inspect the method and decide what additional evidence is needed for their own workflow.`,
    `Operational checklist for interpretation: preserve the original request, mark the source version, identify the reviewer, and record the date of the decision. When the queue changes, do not compare the new result with the old result without noting the changed input, tool, deadline, or authority. Ask whether the observed pattern would remain if the owner changed, if demand increased, or if an exception became common. These questions do not produce a universal score; they make the local evidence more honest and help the team choose a safe next experiment.`,
    `Conclusion: ${title} is most useful when treated as a scoped management question. Begin with a narrow queue, a written boundary, representative examples, individual access, a named reviewer, and a dated review point. Keep final judgment with the internal owner. After the sample, choose one bounded action: continue, clarify, narrow, add coverage, or pause. The evidence should make that choice easier while preserving uncertainty. That is a stronger conclusion than a promise of speed, savings, or suitability based only on a title or a single anecdote.`,
  ];
};

const august17ResearchBody: Record<string, readonly string[]> = {
  'research-filipino-assistant-appointment-scheduling-constraints': [
    'Research question and scope: when can a Filipino assistant prepare appointment scheduling without creating a commitment the business has not approved? This report studies the evidence needed to distinguish a routable request from a request that requires professional, commercial, or customer-service judgment. The unit is a scheduling case, not the number of invitations sent. A case begins with a request and ends when it is confirmed, returned for missing information, or escalated to an owner. The report does not assess one worker, one nationality, or one calendar platform. It examines the queue design, source records, authority, and review conditions that shape a safe result.',
    'Method: sample cases across ordinary appointments, reschedules, cancellations, time-zone changes, and requests with missing details. For each case record the requested service, source of truth, permitted action, deadline, confirmation channel, and stop condition. Separate a prepared option from a confirmed appointment. That distinction matters because an assistant may compare available slots or draft a message while the responsible person still owns availability, fees, policy, consent, and any professional advice. NIST risk-management guidance supports identifying owners and controls, while the FTC data-security guidance is relevant when calendars contain personal information. Neither source supplies a universal scheduling threshold.',
    'The first finding is that invitation volume is a weak measure of useful scheduling. A single request may involve multiple calendars, a date boundary, a protected period, or a participant whose attendance changes the purpose of the meeting. Count unresolved constraints and owner decisions alongside completed cases. A case that reaches the right owner with the relevant context can be a successful escalation. A case that produces a fast invitation with the wrong time, audience, or implied commitment is not a success merely because the calendar changed. Review the source event and the final record together.',
    'A second finding concerns confirmation language. The assistant should use approved wording that identifies the proposed time, participants, location or link, and any detail the requester must verify. The message should not imply that a service is guaranteed, that a professional has accepted a case, or that a business has agreed to a commercial term unless the owner has already approved it. When the request is ambiguous, preserve the minimum context needed for routing. Do not copy sensitive notes into a general scheduling thread simply because they appeared in the original request.',
    'The useful comparison is between stable scheduling cases and judgment-heavy cases. Stable cases have a known calendar, a defined duration, approved participants, and a clear confirmation rule. Judgment-heavy cases include competing priorities, private events, travel disruption, unusual customer requests, or a change that affects a promise. They should be measured separately because combining them makes a queue look predictable while hiding the review burden. A manager can assign preparation for both categories, but the authority to choose among conflicting commitments remains with the accountable owner.',
    'Time-zone evidence needs care. A calendar may display a local time while the participants work from different locations or cross a daylight-saving transition. Record the time zone used by the source system, the time zone communicated to each participant, and the date shown after conversion. Do not infer availability from a job title or a general working-hours statement. A short live sample with realistic dates is more informative than a claim that a person can handle scheduling in the abstract. The sample should include at least one date-boundary case before wider access is considered.',
    'Limitations: a scheduling sample can miss seasonal demand, last-minute cancellations, platform outages, and changes in owner preference. Calendar data may also contain legacy errors or private information that should not be exported for analysis. Observed correction rates describe the sampled queue and its instructions; they do not predict every future case. A reviewer should record the population, dates, exclusions, and unresolved questions. If the team cannot state those boundaries, the honest result is that the scheduling role needs more scoping, not that the assistant is or is not suitable.',
    'Conclusion: appointment scheduling is a bounded support lane when the source of truth, approved action, confirmation wording, and stop rule are explicit. Start with preparation and low-risk confirmations, use individual access, and review cases that cross a time zone, privacy boundary, or business commitment. Measure correct routing, context completeness, and owner decision time rather than invitations alone. This evidence can support a local decision about scope. It cannot establish a universal promise about speed, availability, or service outcomes.',
  ],
  'research-outsourced-assistant-document-accessibility-review': [
    'Research question and scope: what evidence shows that an outsourced assistant can review document accessibility without confusing visual polish with usable access? The unit of analysis is a document and its audience, not a formatting task counted in isolation. This report focuses on headings, reading order, links, tables, contrast, labels, and preserved meaning. It does not certify conformance for every document or replace an accessibility specialist. It asks how a support queue can identify observable issues, record evidence, and route uncertain or high-impact defects to the document owner.',
    'Method: select documents that resemble the real service, including a short memo, a longer report, a table-heavy file, and a document exported to PDF. Record the source format, intended audience, authoring tool, required reading order, and known constraints. Test the rendered artifact, not only the editor view. WCAG 2.2 provides the relevant success criteria, but a checklist copied from the standard is not a research finding. The reviewer should name the criterion or observable failure, preserve a small example, and explain whether the assistant may correct it or should escalate.',
    'The first finding is that structural defects can survive a visually clean review. A document can have consistent fonts and spacing while its heading hierarchy, table headers, link text, or reading order remains unclear to someone using assistive technology. Record structural checks separately from appearance checks. The result should identify what was tested and in which tool. “Looks correct” is not a reproducible method. A second reviewer should be able to reopen the file, follow the same path, and see why the item passed, failed, or required specialist judgment.',
    'The second finding is that meaning must be protected during formatting. An assistant may repair a heading level, add descriptive link text from approved source material, or correct a table structure when the expected result is clear. The assistant should not rewrite a legal, medical, contractual, or policy statement to make it shorter or easier without an authorized owner. If a table is visually dense because its meaning depends on merged cells, the safe action may be to escalate rather than simplify it. Accessibility work is therefore partly a content-ownership question, not only a design question.',
    'Compare files by audience and consequence, not by page count. An internal status note, a customer-facing instruction, and a regulated form may require different review depth. Record whether the file is a source of decision, a temporary draft, or a published artifact. A defect in a private working note has a different route from a defect in an externally used document, even when the same formatting rule appears to apply. This distinction keeps a support queue practical and prevents a general formatting capability claim from being mistaken for an accessibility conformance claim.',
    'The review record should include the original file, the rendered output, the checks performed, the tool and version where relevant, and the owner who accepts the result. Do not upload sensitive documents to an unapproved checker. Use separate identities and approved storage, especially when documents contain customer records or internal decisions. The assistant can gather evidence and make bounded corrections; the document owner remains responsible for meaning, audience, publication, and any formal accessibility statement.',
    'Limitations: automated checkers find only some issues, manual checks vary by assistive technology, and a document that passes one format conversion may fail in another. The sample may not include language, layout, or interaction patterns used later in production. WCAG criteria also require contextual judgment in some cases. State which format, audience, tool, and criteria were included. Do not turn a small review into a claim that every document from a team is accessible or that one worker has specialist certification.',
    'Conclusion: document accessibility support is useful when it preserves meaning, names the test, and leaves high-consequence interpretation with the owner. Begin with representative files, a defined audience, approved tools, and a review sample that includes both structure and rendered behavior. Measure reproducible findings and safe escalation, not the number of files touched. The evidence supports a scoped service decision, not a blanket conformance guarantee.',
  ],
  'research-virtual-assistant-customer-follow-up-recontact': [
    'Research question and scope: does a customer follow-up queue reduce uncertainty when it tracks ownership and promised next steps, or does it simply increase message volume? This report examines recontact as an operational signal. A case is the original question plus the approved follow-up record, and the outcome is resolved, waiting on an owner, returned for context, or escalated. The report does not claim that recontact proves poor service or that one assistant caused it. It asks what evidence a manager should inspect before changing a support role or communication rule.',
    'Method: sample closed and open cases across status requests, missing documents, appointment reminders, and cases with an exception. Record the original promise, source record, due date, approved language, send time, owner, and next action. Mark whether the customer contacted the team again before resolution and why. Separate a customer recontact caused by a changed request from one caused by a missed or unclear commitment. ILO material is useful context for sustainable work arrangements, while FTC guidance matters when follow-up includes personal information. These sources do not establish a universal service-level target.',
    'The first finding is that message count is a poor proxy for continuity. A concise update with a clear owner can prevent recontact, while several polite messages can prolong uncertainty when nobody owns the underlying decision. Review queue age, missing inputs, returned drafts, and unresolved owner actions together. The assistant may send an approved reminder or assemble context for an owner. The assistant should not invent a new promise, interpret a complaint, approve a remedy, or imply that silence means acceptance.',
    'The second finding is that recontact needs a reason code that a reviewer can understand. Useful categories include no response by the promised date, unclear next step, changed customer request, missing internal decision, or channel failure. Avoid labels such as “difficult customer” that describe a person instead of the service condition. The categories should be few enough to use consistently and specific enough to trigger an action. When the reason is uncertain, preserve the uncertainty and route it rather than turning an assumption into a customer record.',
    'Follow-up also depends on the source of truth. A note in chat may not be authoritative if the customer record, appointment system, or approved case system has changed. The assistant should cite the current record and identify what was last approved. If the source conflicts, stop the message and escalate with minimal context. The owner can decide whether the record should be corrected, whether the customer needs a new explanation, or whether an applicable policy governs the next step. The queue should not solve ownership gaps through increasingly frequent reminders.',
    'Compare cases by consequence and review burden. A low-risk status request can use a narrow template; a disputed charge, privacy request, complaint, or account change needs a different route. Count the time needed to reconstruct context and decide an exception. A queue that looks efficient on sent messages may consume more management time when every case is returned. Record accepted, corrected, escalated, and recontacted cases separately so a manager can see whether the rule is improving continuity or only moving work between channels.',
    'Limitations: recontact depends on customer expectations, channel availability, language, policy, and events outside the support queue. A small sample cannot prove satisfaction or causation. The same customer may recontact because a new issue appeared, not because the first follow-up failed. State the period, case types, exclusions, and definition of resolution. Never use nationality, accent, or location as a proxy for communication quality. Evaluate the record, the approved message, and the ownership path instead.',
    'Conclusion: follow-up support is ready for a defined queue when promises, owners, due dates, source records, and stop conditions are visible. Track recontact as a question about continuity, not as a verdict on a worker. Start with approved, reversible communication and keep remedies, policy interpretation, and new commitments with accountable staff. The evidence can guide a local change to the queue or review rule, but it cannot guarantee customer outcomes.',
  ],
  'research-remote-assistant-vendor-record-reconciliation': [
    'Research question and scope: when can a remote assistant reconcile vendor records from approved sources without making a tax, payment, contract, or identity decision? This report studies record comparison, not vendor selection. The unit is a vendor record pair and the evidence connecting them. A case can be matched, left unresolved, returned for missing evidence, or escalated. The goal is to measure data quality and review burden while keeping merge, deletion, banking, and contractual decisions with an authorized owner.',
    'Method: build a sample from the actual systems used by the business and label the source authority for each field. Compare names, identifiers, addresses, contacts, status, dates, and attachments only where the assistant has permission to view them. Record exact matches, possible matches, conflicts, and no-match cases. NIST Cybersecurity Framework guidance supports identifying data and responsible owners; FTC data-security guidance supports limiting access to what the task requires. Neither source says that a similarity score is proof of identity.',
    'The first finding is that duplicate detection and record merging are different actions. A possible match can be safely flagged with the evidence that produced the flag. A merge changes the system of record and may affect payment, tax, access, reporting, or the history of a business relationship. Keep those actions in separate permissions and separate measures. A queue can produce useful work when it improves the owner’s review without silently deciding that two records belong to the same entity.',
    'The second finding is that field completeness does not establish correctness. A record may contain every field and still carry an outdated address, a copied contact, or a value imported from an untrusted source. Record field provenance and update period when the decision depends on them. Where sources disagree, preserve both values, name the conflict, and route the decision. The assistant should not “clean” a conflict by choosing the most recent-looking value unless the owner has defined that rule and its exceptions.',
    'Measure the queue with a confusion table: confirmed match, confirmed non-match, unresolved, returned, and owner override. Review false positives and false negatives from a deliberate sample. If the business cannot label the outcome without reconstructing the whole relationship, the source data or authority rule is not ready for broad delegation. A small sample of ordinary records is insufficient; include subsidiaries, renamed vendors, shared addresses, and records with incomplete identifiers when those conditions exist in the real queue.',
    'Access and retention are part of reconciliation quality. Use individual accounts, least privilege, approved storage, and a clear rule for temporary exports. Do not copy bank details, tax identifiers, or personal contact information into a general handoff when a record link and minimal reason are enough. Close temporary access when the comparison ends. The owner must approve any edit that changes the authoritative record or triggers a financial or contractual process.',
    'Limitations: a record sample can be distorted by legacy duplicates, changing vendor names, missing source history, and differences between systems. A matching rule that works for one source pair may fail for another. A review result describes the sample and its controls, not the reliability of every remote assistant or every vendor database. Publish the population, fields, period, thresholds, and exclusions so a reader can judge whether the finding applies to a similar queue.',
    'Conclusion: vendor-record reconciliation is a useful support lane when matching evidence is visible and irreversible decisions remain with the owner. Start with flagging and source capture, test false positives and unresolved cases, and widen authority only after the review path is stable. Measure evidence quality and owner decision time, not the number of merges. The method supports a scoped delegation decision without turning data cleanup into an unsupported identity claim.',
  ],
  'research-filipino-assistant-project-status-signal-quality': [
    'Research question and scope: which project-status signals help an owner decide what needs attention, and which merely make activity look complete? This report examines status evidence prepared by a Filipino assistant for distributed project work. The unit is a status item tied to a source record, owner, date, and decision. It does not rank workers or claim that a dashboard predicts delivery. It asks whether a status brief distinguishes verified progress, open work, blocked work, and interpretation that still belongs with the project owner.',
    'Method: sample updates from routine work, a delayed item, a dependency, a changed requirement, and a completed deliverable. For each update record the source, last-change date, stated owner, next action, evidence of completion, and unresolved question. Compare the brief with the underlying task, document, or decision record. NIST governance principles support assigning responsibility and understanding context, but they do not turn a status label into evidence. The reviewer should mark whether a statement is sourced fact, calculation, local observation, or recommendation.',
    'The first finding is that “on track” is not a reproducible result without a baseline. The reader needs the milestone, expected date, current evidence, dependency, and condition that would change the status. A support assistant can gather those fields and flag a variance. The project owner decides whether the variance changes scope, sequence, resourcing, or the delivery promise. This boundary prevents a neat summary from hiding a decision that nobody has formally made.',
    'The second finding is that freshness matters. A status note assembled from a stale tracker can be accurate about what was true when the record was last updated and still mislead a decision made today. Record the source timestamp and the time the status was prepared. When the owner has not updated a dependency, say so. Do not fill the gap with a confident narrative. A visible unknown is more useful than a plausible cause that cannot be traced to evidence.',
    'Signal quality also depends on negative space. A report should show items with no current owner, missing acceptance evidence, repeated return, or a dependency that has passed its date. These are not proof of failure, but they are reasons for review. Avoid measuring the assistant by the number of status lines produced. Measure whether a reader can locate the source, understand the uncertainty, and identify the next owner action without reopening every conversation.',
    'Use separate access for gathering and changing project records. The assistant may assemble a brief, link approved artifacts, and request an update. The assistant should not rewrite a milestone, close a task, approve scope, or promise a new date unless an authorized owner has supplied that decision. If a project record contains confidential customer or personnel information, keep the handoff minimal and use approved storage. A clear status method includes the boundary, not only the fields.',
    'Limitations: project systems differ, updates can lag, and a small sample may overrepresent visible work. A status signal cannot prove causation, delivery quality, or customer satisfaction. It may also reflect a manager’s definition of complete rather than the recipient’s. State the project type, observation period, source systems, and excluded work. Do not generalize a local reporting method to every project or use nationality as an explanation for a signal.',
    'Conclusion: a status brief earns trust when each statement points to evidence and each unknown has an owner. Begin with a small set of decisions the brief must support, keep source dates visible, and separate preparation from project authority. The evidence can justify a clearer reporting lane or a better review cadence. It cannot justify a universal productivity claim or a promise that a dashboard will predict project outcomes.',
  ],
  'research-outsourced-assistant-knowledge-base-change-control': [
    'Research question and scope: how can an outsourced assistant maintain a knowledge base without quietly changing policy, technical meaning, or customer commitments? This report treats a knowledge-base entry as a controlled record with an owner, source, audience, version, and review condition. The unit is a proposed change, not an article count. A change can be accepted, returned, escalated, or rejected as stale. The report does not claim that a tidy library is accurate. It asks what evidence lets a manager distinguish maintenance from unauthorized interpretation.',
    'Method: sample new guidance, correction requests, links that no longer work, and entries with conflicting owner instructions. Record the request source, authoritative document, effective date, affected audience, proposed wording, reviewer, and retirement condition. Google Search Central guidance supports useful, people-first information but is not proof of internal policy correctness. NIST and FTC guidance are relevant when entries describe security or personal-data handling. The owner of the policy or process must approve material meaning; the assistant can prepare the change record and identify gaps.',
    'The first finding is that freshness and authority are different. A newer note is not automatically the governing instruction if it was informal, incomplete, or written for another audience. Every entry needs a source of truth and a rule for what happens when sources conflict. Record the effective date and the reviewer’s decision. Do not resolve ambiguity by choosing the document with the newest filename or the most confident prose. A knowledge base should expose uncertainty rather than convert it into a new rule.',
    'The second finding is that change control needs a reason for retirement. Links break, tools change, roles move, and temporary instructions outlive the incident that created them. Mark whether an entry is superseded, corrected, merged, or no longer applicable. Preserve a pointer to the replacement where appropriate, but do not keep duplicate guidance merely to avoid a blank search result. The assistant may identify candidates for retirement; the accountable owner decides whether the business can safely remove or replace the instruction.',
    'Test retrieval as well as writing. Ask whether a user can find the approved entry from the terms they actually use, then check whether the first result contains the audience, scope, exception, and effective date. Search performance alone does not establish correctness. A useful sample includes a new employee question, a familiar routine question, and an edge case. Record which answer was found, which source supported it, and whether an owner had to clarify the interpretation.',
    'Keep access narrow. Use an individual account, approved repository, version history, and a review path that prevents direct publication of policy changes. The assistant should not create a new approval requirement, promise an outcome, expose restricted records, or give professional advice while “improving” an article. If the proposed edit changes rights, commitments, security controls, or customer instructions, stop and route it with the minimum context needed for the decision.',
    'Limitations: a small sample cannot represent every audience, language, tool, or seasonal policy change. Search terms and user questions evolve. A link check cannot verify that the destination still means what the entry says. State the sources, dates, audiences, and checks included. A maintained knowledge base can still contain incorrect source material, so the result should be interpreted as evidence about the control process, not a guarantee of truth.',
    'Conclusion: outsourced maintenance is safe when every change has a source, owner, effective date, and disposition. Start with link and metadata checks, add bounded wording changes, and keep policy interpretation with the owner. Measure accepted changes, returned proposals, stale entries, and unresolved conflicts. That evidence can justify a local knowledge-base role. It cannot support a blanket claim that maintenance alone makes a team informed or compliant.',
  ],
  'research-virtual-assistant-recruiting-scheduling-fairness': [
    'Research question and scope: what can a virtual assistant do to reduce recruiting scheduling friction while preserving candidate privacy and a fair selection process? This report studies coordination evidence, not candidate quality. The unit is a scheduling case and its handling time, missing information, accommodation path, and ownership. The assistant may coordinate approved times and maintain status fields. The recruiting team retains assessment, selection, rejection, compensation, and interpretation of candidate evidence.',
    'Method: sample interview requests, reschedules, cancellations, time-zone conversions, and accommodation-related messages using the same approved fields. Record when the request arrived, what information was available, which options were offered, whether the candidate needed a different channel or accommodation, and who decided the next step. Keep personal data to the minimum needed for coordination. ILO materials provide context for decent work, but they do not define the organization’s hiring obligations. Applicable law and internal policy must remain with the responsible recruiting owner.',
    'The first finding is that speed alone is not fairness. A fast scheduling response can still omit a requested accommodation, misstate a time zone, or expose a candidate’s private information. Review completeness, correction, and safe routing alongside elapsed time. A support queue should make it easy to request clarification without penalizing the person for having a non-standard schedule or communication need. The assistant should use approved, neutral language and avoid recording impressions about accent, personality, background, or presumed availability.',
    'The second finding is that standardized fields improve auditability only when they are not used to erase context. Capture the agreed interview type, participants, duration, time zone, confirmation status, and next owner. Store accommodation information in the approved restricted location rather than in a broad scheduling note. If a participant’s request affects interview design or process fairness, route it to the recruiting owner. The assistant should not decide that a request is inconvenient, excessive, or evidence of fit.',
    'Compare cases by the source of delay. Some waits come from interviewer availability, some from missing job information, some from candidate choice, and some from an internal approval. Do not attribute all elapsed time to the scheduling worker. A useful report shows the queue state and the responsible dependency. Include corrected and cancelled cases so the measure does not reward scheduling an event that later needed repair. The evidence should help the recruiting team fix the process rather than judge a person from a single timestamp.',
    'Access boundaries matter because recruiting records are sensitive. Use individual identities, least privilege, approved scheduling tools, and clear retention rules. Do not share resumes, interview notes, or accommodation details in informal channels. The assistant can prepare options, send approved confirmations, and update status. Hiring judgment, evaluation evidence, candidate disposition, and any exception to the approved process remain with the recruiting owner.',
    'Limitations: scheduling data cannot establish selection fairness, candidate experience, or compliance by itself. Small samples may miss unusual schedules, language needs, system outages, or high-volume periods. Definitions of response time also vary. State the period, case types, exclusions, and source systems. Do not use country, nationality, or a communication style as a proxy for capability. Interpret evidence at the level of coordination control and process ownership.',
    'Conclusion: recruiting coordination is a bounded support lane when the fields, language, privacy rules, and escalation owner are explicit. Measure complete and respectful coordination, not speed alone. Start with approved cases and representative exceptions, then review whether the process preserves candidate choice and internal accountability. The evidence supports a local scheduling design decision, not a conclusion about candidate quality or a universal hiring outcome.',
  ],
  'research-remote-assistant-travel-option-comparability': [
    'Research question and scope: when does a remote assistant’s travel research produce options that a traveler can actually compare? The unit is an option set tied to a complete brief, not the number of links collected. This report examines dates, route constraints, transfer time, policy limits, cancellation conditions, and source freshness. It does not recommend a destination or guarantee an itinerary. It asks whether the research record makes trade-offs visible while payment, risk acceptance, and final booking remain with the traveler or authorized owner.',
    'Method: sample trips with different dates, time zones, connection requirements, traveler preferences, and policy constraints. Record the request as received, the search period, source links, displayed local times, baggage or transfer assumptions, cancellation terms, and unresolved questions. Compare like with like and label values that cannot be compared. A source-backed option is not a reservation. Travel inventory changes, so the record needs a timestamp and a rule for rechecking before an owner acts.',
    'The first finding is that more options can reduce decision quality when the brief is incomplete. A list of ten routes is less useful than three comparable options with total travel time, connection risk, policy fit, and a clearly stated unknown. The assistant can gather and normalize approved fields. The traveler decides whether a long transfer, restrictive fare, or late arrival is acceptable. Do not infer a preference from an old itinerary or treat a frequently chosen route as a standing approval.',
    'The second finding is that local time must be shown with the date. Overnight travel and daylight-saving changes can shift arrival dates, and a connection that looks short on a summary page may not be feasible after terminal changes or required check-in. Record the source’s time zone and the converted local time used in the comparison. If the source is ambiguous, flag it. A polished table should not hide a missing connection rule or imply that an assistant accepted travel risk on the owner’s behalf.',
    'Compare options on decision-relevant fields: constraint fit, total elapsed time, transfer count, changeability, source freshness, and policy exception. Keep price out of this research contract’s public copy, but a private owner may still have an approved budget field in the actual booking process. The assistant should not publish or promise rates, nor should it purchase or change a reservation. The public conclusion concerns comparability and authority, not a travel deal or guaranteed savings.',
    'Use approved tools and limit access to traveler information. Do not copy passport, health, payment, or loyalty details into a general brief. Store sensitive fields only where authorized and retain them only as long as the workflow requires. If a carrier rule, visa question, or health requirement needs professional or current interpretation, route it to the traveler or qualified owner. The assistant can identify the question and source, not decide the legal or personal risk.',
    'Limitations: live inventory, weather, carrier disruption, policy changes, and traveler preference can invalidate a comparison quickly. Search results may omit fees or conditions, and a sample cannot prove that a route will remain available. State the search date, sources, assumptions, and exclusions. Do not generalize one travel researcher’s result to all trips or treat an organized option set as a booking confirmation.',
    'Conclusion: travel research is useful when the brief is complete, options share comparable fields, source times are visible, and the traveler owns acceptance. Start with a small set of approved constraints and a recheck rule. Measure missing assumptions and correction burden, not link count. The evidence supports a local research and handoff design, not a promise about availability, safety, or outcome.',
  ],
  'research-filipino-assistant-work-queue-interruption-cost': [
    'Research question and scope: how should a team measure interruption cost in a support queue before deciding whether a Filipino assistant can absorb more recurring work? The unit is a work item plus the interruption and recovery it creates, not a simple count of messages. This report studies arrival pattern, context switching, deadline pressure, rework, and owner intervention. It does not claim that remote work or a country of residence causes interruption. It asks which queue conditions make a role hard to review and which controls reduce avoidable switching.',
    'Method: observe a defined period that includes ordinary demand and at least one busy period. Record arrivals, estimated effort, due dates, category, interruption source, time to resume, returned work, and owner decisions. Distinguish a genuine urgent exception from a message marked urgent by habit. A queue may be interrupt-driven because its service promise is unclear, its source data is incomplete, or its owner changes priority without recording why. The evidence should identify the condition rather than blame the person receiving the work.',
    'The first finding is that arrival volume alone does not describe interruption cost. Five small requests from different systems can demand more context switching than five similar requests from one queue. Group work by source, required tool, decision owner, and deadline. Record whether the interruption was handled, deferred with a visible reason, or escalated. This allows a manager to see which work can be batched and which genuinely needs immediate attention without turning constant availability into a quality measure.',
    'The second finding is that rework is part of the cost. An item returned because the brief omitted an approval field may consume more time than its original handling. Count clarification, correction, and owner review separately from task completion. If a support role appears slow because it stops at the right boundary, widening authority may make the system less safe. A better response may be a clearer intake record, a protected focus window, or a rule that groups low-risk work before review.',
    'Compare queues by reversibility and consequence. Routine document preparation can often wait or be corrected. A customer commitment, access change, payment issue, or safety concern may need a faster owner route. The assistant can classify and prepare within the rule set, but should not silently reorder work that changes commitments. Use a visible priority field with a source and owner. Review whether urgent labels predict actual consequence or merely reflect sender preference.',
    'Time-zone coverage is a dependency, not a productivity verdict. An overnight handoff may be useful for preparation but poor for a decision that needs immediate context. Record when an item became ready, when the owner could review it, and whether the gap caused rework. A schedule should state the coverage window, backup, and stop rule. Do not infer that a person is available because a status indicator is green or because a general shift overlaps part of the day.',
    'Limitations: interruption measures are sensitive to logging quality, seasonal demand, tool notifications, and manager behavior. A short study may miss rare emergencies. The observed queue may change when people know it is being measured. State the period, definitions, exclusions, and whether owner time was sampled directly. Do not translate a local interruption pattern into a claim about individual discipline, nationality, or universal capacity.',
    'Conclusion: a recurring queue is ready to expand when its arrivals, priority rules, review burden, and interruption costs are visible. Start with bounded work, batch what can wait, and create a clear owner path for consequences that cannot. Measure recovery and rework as well as completion. The evidence supports a local capacity or queue-design decision, not a guarantee of speed or a judgment about a worker.',
  ],
  'research-outsourced-assistant-approval-delay-attribution': [
    'Research question and scope: how can a team tell whether approval delay comes from the assistant’s preparation, the owner’s decision queue, or an unclear rule? This report studies elapsed time across a support workflow. The unit is a request from complete intake to recorded decision, with work time and waiting time separated. It does not treat a slow decision as evidence of poor performance by any one person. It asks what timestamps and causes make the delay actionable without shifting authority to the assistant.',
    'Method: select requests that need an owner decision, including ordinary approvals, missing-context returns, conflicting instructions, and exceptions. Record when the request arrived, when it became complete, when preparation finished, when the owner received it, when a question was answered, and when the decision was recorded. Use one definition of each timestamp. NIST governance guidance supports explicit responsibility and context; ILO material can inform sustainable work design. Neither source can identify the cause of a particular company’s delay without local evidence.',
    'The first finding is that “approval time” often combines different waits. A request may sit because the assistant has not found the source, because the owner is unavailable, because the approver lacks context, or because the policy does not say who decides. Report those states separately. A short preparation step followed by a long owner queue is not the same problem as a draft that requires several rounds of clarification. The remedy depends on the state, so a single average hides the decision the manager needs to make.',
    'The second finding is that complete handoffs reduce attribution error. A useful handoff names the question, evidence, permitted action, recommendation if allowed, risk, deadline, and exact decision needed. It should also state what the assistant did not decide. If the owner returns the item, record the reason and whether the brief or source record changed. Do not label a returned item as assistant failure when the owner supplied a new constraint after preparation began.',
    'Compare delays by request class and consequence. A routine document approval may use a scheduled review window. A customer-impacting exception or access concern may need a named backup owner. The assistant can flag age, assemble context, and send an approved reminder. The assistant should not approve by default, change a deadline without authority, or treat silence as consent. A safe queue makes the next owner action explicit and stops when the decision is outside the role.',
    'Measure rework and escalation completeness. A fast handoff that omits the source can create a slower decision after the owner asks for reconstruction. A slower handoff that contains the right evidence may reduce total elapsed time. Review representative cases, including those that were approved quickly and those that remained open. Keep private or sensitive detail in approved systems and put only the necessary routing context in the handoff. The goal is to improve flow without broadening access or authority by accident.',
    'Limitations: timestamps may be inconsistent, owners may batch decisions, and the sample may coincide with a holiday, launch, or unusual absence. A local correlation between complete handoffs and shorter delays does not prove causation. State the period, request types, time-zone assumptions, and exclusions. Do not generalize the result to every outsourced arrangement or use delay as a proxy for a person’s commitment or capability.',
    'Conclusion: approval delay becomes manageable when the workflow separates preparation, waiting, review, and decision. Start with clear timestamp definitions, a complete handoff, a named owner and backup, and a stop rule for unresolved authority. Measure total decision flow and the reason for each wait. The evidence can support a local review cadence or role boundary, but it cannot guarantee faster approvals or assign blame from elapsed time alone.',
  ],
};

