const makeAug17Depth = (slug: string, title: string): string[] => {
  const [focus, measure, examples, warning, source] = august17ResearchDepth[slug];
  return [
    `Research question and scope: how should a team evaluate ${focus} when deciding whether a Philippines-based assistant can support a recurring operations queue? This report examines ${measure} across ${examples}. It does not rank workers, predict an individual result, or treat a job title as a workload definition. The unit of analysis is a dated queue and its review conditions. Any manager using the finding should name the source system, observation period, excluded cases, reviewer, and decision that remains internal.`,
    `Methodology: the analysis separates a reported fact, a local observation, a calculation, and a recommendation. Start with a small sample containing ordinary items and deliberately selected exceptions. For each item, preserve the input, permitted action, expected finish condition, evidence used, review result, and escalation reason. This makes the comparison inspectable by someone who did not perform the work. A sample can illuminate queue design and review burden; it cannot establish a universal performance claim about Filipino assistants or outsourced support.`,
    `The central operating distinction is between preparing information and deciding what the business commits to. An assistant may organize records, draft within approved language, identify conflicts, or prepare options when the brief permits it. The internal owner should retain final approvals, payment actions, policy changes, legal interpretation, account recovery, and unusual customer commitments. This boundary is material to ${focus}: the measured output is not useful if the reviewer cannot tell whether an action was merely prepared or actually authorized.`,
    `For measurement, define the numerator and denominator before collecting results. Record accepted, returned, escalated, owner-overridden, and unresolved items separately. Measure review time alongside handling time, because a queue that looks fast may create a longer decision delay when every result needs reconstruction. Where a percentage or average is derived, retain the inputs, period, unit, and exclusions. ${source} provides relevant principles for this topic, but it does not supply a universal benchmark for OutsourcedAssistants.com or for a particular client queue.`,
    `A useful comparison looks at input stability, exception rate, consequence of error, reversibility, and review effort. Input stability asks whether the source arrives in a predictable form. Exception rate asks how often the written rule is insufficient. Consequence asks who could be affected and how quickly an error can be contained. Reversibility asks whether the owner can safely undo the action. Review effort asks how much context is needed to verify it. These dimensions explain why ${warning} and why completion volume alone is a weak decision signal.`,
    `The evidence should also account for access and privacy. Use individual accounts, least-privilege permissions, approved storage, and a named owner for exceptions. Do not copy unnecessary personal or customer information into a second note merely to make a handoff look complete. If the task crosses a boundary, preserve the minimum routing context and stop the unsupported action. Repeated exceptions are evidence that the queue or permission design needs revision, not permission to widen authority informally.`,
    `Interpretation must stay conditional. A narrow queue with stable inputs, representative examples, and a responsive reviewer may be suitable for an initial assignment. That observation does not prove fit for a different tool, season, deadline, owner, or risk level. A returned item may indicate an unclear brief rather than an unsuitable person; an escalation may show that the stop rule worked. Compare patterns over a defined period and state the alternative explanations before changing scope.`,
    `A second test should change one condition at a time: input format, deadline, reviewer, system, or exception type. If the result changes because the source changed, record source freshness or lineage. If it changes because the reviewer was unavailable, record coverage dependency. If it changes because the instruction relied on private context, add an example or an explicit question field. This produces a better management decision than treating one smooth sample as proof that the operating design is complete.`,
    `For teams considering outsourced assistants, the practical implication is to begin with a bounded queue whose records are visible and whose errors are containable. Give the person enough context to complete the permitted action, but do not hide the owner’s decision inside a vague phrase such as “handle as needed.” Set a review date before adding volume. Keep a short record of corrections and unresolved questions so later conclusions can distinguish a training issue, a source problem, a capacity problem, and a boundary problem.`,
    `There are limitations. A small sample may miss seasonal demand, tool outages, language ambiguity, rare high-consequence cases, or changes in policy. Reviewer judgment can vary, and legacy records may already be wrong. Time-zone observations can be confounded by meeting schedules; document results can be confounded by unstable version naming; research findings can be confounded by stale sources. State the population, dates, exclusions, and unresolved questions. Do not generalize from one client, one queue, or one worker to an entire national workforce or service category.`,
    `The conclusion is evidence-led but bounded: ${title} is useful as a local operating decision about inputs, authority, review, and risk. It should lead to one of five actions after the sample: continue the scope, clarify the brief, narrow permissions, add review coverage, or pause. The evidence does not justify promises about speed, savings, availability, quality, or suitability without a defined comparison and a trustworthy record. A clear boundary is itself a finding because it shows what the support lane can and cannot responsibly carry.`,
  ];
};

